
/* ============ THE MO'S BOOK — generated from book.md by mkbook.py. Edit book.md, not this file. ============ */
const BOOK=[{"t": "Start Here — read this first", "plan": "", "h": "<p>Mo's A Place For Steaks · Greenwood, Indiana · Est. 1999</p><p>This book follows the original Mo's Training Itinerary — the same plan that opened this restaurant, taught in the same order, because that order was right. Ten days of material, about eighteen hours of training when it ran live. Read it front to back and you have had the same education the opening team had, with everything we have learned since folded in.</p><p>Where we have the material, it is in here. Where we do not have it yet, you will see a line that starts with <b>STILL TO GET</b> — that is not a hole, that is the plan. We are going to get it.</p><p>The app is the living version of this book. Prices change, specials rotate, schedules move — the app keeps up. The book teaches you how to think. The app keeps you current.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">How we speak — the verbiage that makes it fine dining</h4><p>Before day one of the menu, learn this. The words are the uniform of the sentence. The same fact, said the Mo's way, sells instead of scares. Never lie — reframe.</p><ul class=\"steps\"><li>A steak is never \"tough.\" It is <b>\"less tender\"</b> or \"a firmer bite with more chew and more flavor.\"</li><li>Nothing is \"fatty.\" It is <b>\"richly marbled,\" \"buttery,\" \"decadent.\"</b> Fat is flavor here — talk about it like treasure.</li><li>Nothing is \"cheap.\" It is <b>\"approachable\"</b> or \"a great value.\" Nothing is \"expensive.\" It is <b>\"premium,\" \"our top shelf,\"</b> or simply worth describing until the price makes sense.</li><li>We never say \"I don't know.\" We say <b>\"Great question — let me find out for you,\"</b> and then we actually find out.</li><li>Never \"no problem.\" Say <b>\"my pleasure\"</b> or <b>\"absolutely.\"</b></li><li>They are <b>guests</b>, never customers. We <b>prepare</b> food, we do not \"make\" it. A dish is <b>plated</b>, not \"put together.\"</li><li>Nothing is \"sold out.\" We have <b>\"sold through it tonight\"</b> — 86'd is an inside word and it stays inside.</li><li>Do not say \"just\" — you are never \"just checking in.\" Say <b>\"How is everything tasting for us?\"</b></li><li>A guest is never \"wrong\" about a steak temp. Offer the fix: <b>\"Let me get that closer to perfect for you.\"</b></li><li>Do not oversell with \"amazing\" on everything. Pick specific words — <b>crispy, silky, smoky, bright, rich</b> — and save the big words for the big items.</li><li>Spanish, French, and Italian menu words get said with confidence. Mispronounced confidence beats mumbled fear, but the vocabulary chapter gives you the real pronunciations where they matter.</li><li>When in doubt, describe <b>texture and feeling</b>, not chemistry: \"it melts,\" \"it crackles,\" \"the sauce clings to the pasta.\"</li></ul>"}, {"t": "Day 1 — Orientation", "plan": "Original plan: introductions, points of passion, why we do things our specific way, onboarding paperwork, vocabulary. About three hours.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Welcome to Mo's</h4><p>One idea runs the whole building: <b>leave people better than you found them.</b> Every policy, every step of service, every rule in this book is that idea wearing work clothes.</p><p>The house philosophy, straight from the handbook: everything we do affects how a guest sees us. Service and products <b>above and beyond the guest's expectations</b> — every employee, chef to coat check, taking pride in every action, so each guest leaves with a positive memory of the Mo's Experience. Restaurants that project the million-dollar image make better profits and stand up to the finest competition. We are that restaurant on the south side.</p><p>Who runs the building: <b>Chef Miguel Garatachea</b> leads the kitchen — use his name when you pitch the food; it adds weight. <b>Mike Pavey</b> is General Manager — shift switches go through the GM only. <b>Craig DeVaney</b> is Assistant General Manager. <b>Lillian Speedy</b> is Director of Human Resources and books every banquet in the building.</p><p>Pre-shift lineup runs at <b>4:15 every day</b> with the manager on duty: teams, floor plan, need-to-knows, soup of the day, specials, and recent reviews. Be there, in uniform, ready.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The Points of Passion</h4><p>These sixteen points are the culture. They get tested, and more importantly they get lived. Learn them as ideas, not flash cards.</p><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>1% Less Is The New Standard.</b> Every time you settle for less than 100%, you operate at a lesser percent. Give only 1% less each week for a year and you are running a restaurant at 48% of expectations.</li><li><b>Applied Knowledge Is Power.</b> It is not how smart you are — it is how you use it. Being prepared and understanding every aspect of your job creates confidence and power.</li><li><b>Every Table, Every Time.</b> It is the little things. Attention to detail and demand for perfection sets us apart. Skipping even one step at a table robs the guest of a true Mo's experience.</li><li><b>Protect Your Environment.</b> This is your house. Create a work environment you are proud of and make sure your teammates do the same. Your job is only as good as you make it.</li><li><b>Proactive vs Reactive.</b> See what could happen and stay ahead of it, especially with guests' needs. Reactive people do not make good service industry employees — they are only looking for a paycheck.</li><li><b>Customized Service.</b> \"Happy 23rd Anniversary\" beats \"Happy Anniversary.\" \"Good luck in Iowa, Jim\" beats \"Good luck.\" The Mo's way, not corporate and cold — no matter how big we grow.</li><li><b>Pennies, Nickels &amp; Dimes.</b> Act like the owner. Pick up the pennies people leave sitting around and turn them into twenty dollar bills. Pull the ramekin out of the trash. Sell the desserts and coffees. It all adds up.</li><li><b>Accountability.</b> Be accountable for your actions and your lack of action, and hold others accountable too. Fear of conflict has no place here.</li><li><b>Building Relationships.</b> Guest, then Regular, then VIP, then Friend. Reach level four through customized service and real effort and you have created a loyal guest for life.</li><li><b>Follow The Recipe.</b> Not your way, not another place's way — the Mo's A Place For Steaks way. Do not deter from what works, from recipe books to your job description.</li><li><b>Integrity.</b> It is what you do when nobody is watching. People of high integrity make great teammates.</li><li><b>Twice As Long To Do It Wrong.</b> Do it right the first time. Laziness only leads to doing it again.</li><li><b>Locked On.</b> Stay engaged with the guest, the product, and the operation. The restaurant is a living machine we can only drive when locked on to every part of it.</li><li><b>Good vs Great.</b> The biggest obstacle to becoming great is settling for good. Good gets no complaints and no compliments. Great gets remembered, talked about, and returned to.</li><li><b>Be Hard On The Standard, Not The Person.</b> Get comfortable with conflict — the standard is what we protect. Do not confuse conflict with confrontation, and never take it personally. No excuses.</li><li><b>\"They Weren't Ready For Us.\"</b> With preparation and pride we should say this after EVERY shift. It is the shift where a table thanks a manager for the performance. Apply all the points and we wow the guest every time.</li></ul><h4 class=\"bkh\">Why we do things our specific way</h4><p>Follow The Recipe is the point that explains all the others. Everything in this book was tested on real guests until it worked — the greet script, the bread rule, the two-bite checkback, the way a tower gets stacked. When you improvise past the recipe, you are gambling the guest's night on a hunch. When the recipe genuinely needs to change, that goes up to a manager, the recipe changes for everyone, and the new way becomes the standard. That is how a restaurant gets better without falling apart.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Onboarding paperwork</h4><p>Handled with a manager on your first day, but know what is coming: Federal Form <b>I-9</b> within three business days, with documents proving identity and work eligibility. New-hire reporting to the state (name, address, Social Security number — the law). Your <b>W-4</b> for withholding. Everyone's first <b>120 days are probationary</b>. After that, discipline runs three steps — verbal warning, written warning, termination — with serious offenses skipping steps. Paychecks come <b>every other Wednesday</b>; the pay period runs Wednesday through Tuesday. The full handbook — harassment policy, leave, safety, dress code, house rules — lives in the app under How We Work, and it is required reading.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the current printed onboarding packet, so this chapter can list exactly what a new hire signs page by page.</div><h4 class=\"bkh\">Vocabulary and vernacular</h4><p>Talk the language of the industry. These are the words the original training sheet called vernacular — all of them live in the app's Vocabulary tab too.</p><h5 class=\"bkh2\">Service and etiquette</h5><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>Mise en place</b> (meez-on-PLAHSS) — \"everything in its place.\" Preparing and organizing before service.</li><li><b>Course</b> — one part of a multi-dish meal: appetizer, main, dessert.</li><li><b>Crumber / crumbing down</b> — removing crumbs from the table between courses. Your crumber is part of the uniform.</li><li><b>Marking the table</b> — setting the flatware and glassware the next course needs.</li><li><b>Clearing</b> — removing dishes in a specific order, from the guest's right side.</li><li><b>Pivot point system</b> — seat numbering that tracks every order to the right guest without asking.</li><li><b>Silent service</b> — non-intrusive serving: discretion and minimal noise.</li><li><b>Coursing</b> — timing dishes so guests receive each course in sync.</li><li><b>Refire</b> — re-cooking or remaking a dish that was incorrect or returned.</li></ul><h5 class=\"bkh2\">Beverage service</h5><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>Sommelier</b> (suh-mel-YAY) — wine steward; expert in pairing and service.</li><li><b>Decanting</b> — pouring wine into a decanter to aerate it and separate sediment.</li><li><b>Varietal</b> — the grape a wine is made from: Merlot, Chardonnay.</li><li><b>Vintage</b> — the year the grapes were harvested.</li><li><b>Body</b> — the weight or fullness of a wine: light, medium, full.</li><li><b>Finish</b> — the taste that lingers after the swallow.</li><li><b>Aperitif</b> — a drink before the meal to open the appetite. <b>Digestif</b> — a drink after, to settle it.</li><li><b>Neat / up / on the rocks</b> — no ice; chilled and strained; over ice.</li><li><b>Flight</b> — a set of wines or spirits served together for tasting.</li><li><b>Corkage fee</b> — the fee for opening a bottle the guest brought in.</li></ul><h5 class=\"bkh2\">Kitchen and culinary</h5><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>Sous chef / chef de partie / garde manger</b> — second in command, station chef, cold-station chef.</li><li><b>À la carte</b> — ordering individual dishes. <b>Prix fixe</b> (pree-FEEKS) — a fixed-price set menu.</li><li><b>Degustation / tasting menu</b> — a multi-course showcase of the chef's specialties.</li><li><b>Reduction</b> — simmering a liquid down to concentrate its flavor.</li><li><b>Emulsion</b> — two unblendable liquids made one: vinaigrette, hollandaise.</li><li><b>Sous vide</b> (soo-VEED) — cooking vacuum-sealed food at precise low temperature. Our Caesar egg gets an hour of it.</li><li><b>Blanch / sear / poach / braise</b> — the everyday fine-dining cooking methods.</li><li><b>Roux</b> (ROO) — butter and flour, the classic thickener. It is why \"cream soup\" so often means gluten.</li><li><b>Umami</b> — the fifth taste: savory, rich depth. Dry-aged beef, bonito, mushrooms.</li><li><b>Mirepoix</b> (meer-PWAH) — onion, carrot, celery. The start of most stocks and soups.</li></ul><h5 class=\"bkh2\">Hospitality and guest experience</h5><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>Guest recovery</b> — correcting a mistake so gracefully it becomes a good memory.</li><li><b>Anticipatory service</b> — seeing the need before the guest asks.</li><li><b>Upselling</b> — guiding the guest to what genuinely improves their night, never pushing.</li><li><b>Table maintenance</b> — the quiet constant resetting that keeps a table beautiful mid-meal.</li><li><b>Body language awareness</b> — reading and mirroring the guest's cues.</li><li><b>Fine dining cadence</b> — the rhythm and pacing of the meal. You control the tempo.</li><li><b>VIP / regular guest handling</b> — the extra layer for repeat and high-value guests.</li></ul><h5 class=\"bkh2\">Common restaurant terms</h5><ul class=\"steps\"><li><b>Brigade</b> — the structured hierarchy of kitchen and service staff.</li><li><b>FOH / BOH</b> — front of house (servers, hosts, bartenders, bussers) and back of house (kitchen and support).</li><li><b>86'd</b> — out of stock or unavailable. Inside word only.</li><li><b>Fire / pick up / hands</b> — kitchen calls: start a dish, it is ready, someone run it.</li><li><b>Expo</b> — the expediter coordinating between kitchen and servers.</li><li><b>Checkback / two-bite rule</b> — return within two bites to make sure the course is right.</li></ul>"}, {"t": "Day 2 — Appetizers, Salads and Soups", "plan": "Original plan: dish descriptions, allergens to be aware of, presentation examples, common alterations, serving utensils.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">How to learn any dish</h4><p>The itinerary asks five questions of every dish, and they are the right five. <b>What is it</b> — ingredients and preparation, in words that sell. <b>What are the allergens</b> — the flags that keep a guest safe. <b>How is it presented</b> — plate, setup, and any show. <b>What can change</b> — the alterations the kitchen will actually do. <b>What does it need</b> — the serving utensil that goes down with it. Learn every dish through those five questions and you can talk about anything on the menu with authority.</p><p>Two rules sit above every allergen answer in this chapter. First: the <b>fryer is shared</b> — one fryer, avocado oil, everything fried touches it, so cross-contact applies to every fried item no matter how clean the recipe reads. Second: the allergy protocol is never optional — ask what kind of allergy it is, ring it in Toast, tell your teammate and back server, tell expo and the chef, tell a manager, and never guarantee anything from a study sheet. The chart guides; the chef confirms.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Starters</h4><p><b>Crispy Ahi Tuna Bites — $18.</b> Crispy sushi rice, avocado, ponzu, cilantro, jalapeno. Served with metal Japanese chopsticks. Flags: fin fish, sesame, soy, egg, capsaicin, gluten — the ponzu is built on regular soy sauce, so it is NOT gluten-free no matter how light it looks. Replaced the old seared blackened ahi appetizer.</p><p><b>Seasonal Oysters — $25.</b> On the half shell with cocktail sauce, mignonette, hot sauce, and raw horseradish. Setup is its own little ceremony: oyster fork, Tabasco, Zesta crackers, cocktail forks, and hot water for the dry-ice presentation — no tongs. Know your oyster talk: warm-water oysters run larger, sweeter, more tender; cold-water run smaller, brinier, firmer. Menu marks GF, but the Zesta crackers ride the setup — pull them for a gluten allergy.</p><p><b>Calamari — $17.</b> Sliced thin, lightly floured, flash-fried with red bell and banana pepper rings — never frozen, so it never turns chewy. Two sauces: spicy citrus aioli, creamy with heat, and kung pao, sweet and savory. The kung pao is a nut sauce — treat it as BOTH peanut and tree nut until the chef pins it down; any nut allergy is a full stop. Shared fryer. Serve with tongs. Pairs beautifully with Sancerre or Sauvignon Blanc — the acid cuts the spice.</p><p><b>Shrimp Cocktail — $26.</b> U-6 tiger shrimp — under six to a pound, that is how big they run — boiled in the shell in a house boil of mirepoix, garlic, lemon, dill, pickling spice, and Old Bay, then chilled and peeled by hand. The fiery cocktail sauce is fresh-grated horseradish, English mustard, sriracha, and Worcestershire — and Worcestershire carries anchovy, so a fish allergy needs to hear about the sauce. The shrimp is the star, not the sauce. Cocktail fork and hot water kettle on the setup.</p><p><b>Goat Cheese Spread — $17.</b> Goat, cream, and ricotta cheeses whipped with basil, rosemary, and a pinch of cayenne, topped with pistachios and a honey drizzle finished at the table, with flash-baked crostini and Granny Smith apple wedges. Built to share. Flags: dairy, gluten, tree nuts, a whisper of capsaicin. Spreading knife goes down with it.</p><p><b>Crab Cake — $18.</b> Maryland style, 95% crab — jumbo lump and special crab with a light egg, Dijon, and mayo binder, finished in panko and plated over the remoulade. The selling point writes itself: it is almost all crab. Flags: crab, shellfish, egg, gluten, capsaicin, allium. Serve with a spatula.</p><p><b>Creamy Spicy Crab Dip — $22.</b> Jumbo lump and colossal crab folded into cream cheese and ricotta with an egg yolk, baked until bubbly. House-made corn tortilla chips with a cajun dusting for scooping. Menu marks GF, but the chips come out of the shared fryer. Serving spoon.</p><p><b>Prime Meatballs — $16.</b> We grind filet, ribeye, and NY strip trim in house every day, mix it with mozzarella, Parmesan, breadcrumbs, basil, and oregano, bake, rest, and finish in a hot ceramic dish with house marinara and melted provolone. Flags: beef, dairy, gluten, egg, allium, nightshade. Serving spoon.</p><p><b>Wagyu Tacos — $25.</b> A5 wagyu filet sliced thin and flash-seared, set in a crispy wonton shell over caramelized onions with chimichurri, microgreens, and balsamic pearls — little pearls that pop like caviar. The sauce is a balsamic glaze. Flags: beef, gluten, soy, allium, egg in the wonton. Tongs.</p><p><b>A5 Nigiri — $35.</b> Torched sushi rice, balsamic pearls, Asian pear wine reduction, crispy leeks, served with metal Japanese chopsticks. Trace alcohol in the reduction; the leeks likely ride the shared fryer.</p><p><b>King Crab Legs — market price.</b> Melted butter and cocktail sauce. Setup: cocktail forks, tongs, butter warmer with clarified butter. Recently around $125 a pound — but ALWAYS quote Toast, never memory.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The Lounge</h4><p><b>Filet Sliders (2) — $18.</b> Provolone, smoked garlic aioli, crispy onion strings, pretzel bun, house chips. <b>Carne Asada Nachos — $25.</b> Marinated steak, cheese, pico, avocado, cilantro sour cream. <b>Prime Beef Burger — $23.</b> Bacon, cheddar, the works, with fries. <b>French Dip Egg Rolls — $15.</b> Au jus marinated shaved steak, three-cheese blend, caramelized onions, creamy horseradish — the wrappers carry both gluten and egg. <b>Chicken Parm Sandwich — $24.</b> Fried breast, provolone, tomato diavolo, garlic toasted bun. <b>Tuna Poke — $29.</b> Sushi rice, avocado, mango, seaweed, calamari salad — that calamari salad means shellfish hides in a tuna dish — spicy citrus sauce, wontons. Every fried item here: shared fryer.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The Towers</h4><p><b>Iced Seafood Tower — $98.</b> Six items — memorize them: king crab legs, shrimp cocktail, lobster salad on brioche, oysters, blackened ahi tuna salad, wonton chips. Service: cocktail forks preset, butter warmer with a tealight, clear the top tier once it finishes. The crackers, brioche, and wontons all ride separate — hold those three and the tower itself goes gluten-free. That is a fact that wins tables.</p><p><b>Roasted Seafood Tower — $190.</b> King crab legs, scallops, lobster meat, shrimp, mussels in diavolo, with a seafood-butter sauce pooling underneath — garlic butter, lobster stock, parsley, a touch of house marinara. Service is the show: stacking rack in the base, stack it at the table over a black linen, cocktail forks preset, butter warmer with a tealight. HALFWAY through, clear the shells and stir the pasta setup into that remaining sauce — the house calls the result capavetti pasta, and the tableside pasta moment is the whole reason the tower feels like $190. Do not skip it. This one belongs to the back server.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Soups</h4><p>How soups run: <b>one soup of the day per shift, and it comes complimentary with entrees</b> — or the guest can upcharge $4 to lobster bisque or baked French onion. Bisque is on every day. Verify the exact soup before answering any allergen question, every shift.</p><p><b>Lobster Bisque — $11.</b> Lobster stock built into a roux with tomato, white wine, and sherry vinegar. Flags: shellfish, gluten, dairy, alcohol, nightshade.</p><p><b>Baked French Onion — $13.</b> Vidalia onions caramelized with butter, deglazed with red wine, in a beef bone stock built on mirepoix and herbs over 48 to 72 hours. Brown ceramic crock, herb crouton, provolone broiled until brown and bubbly. Flags: dairy, allium, gluten, alcohol, MSG, beef.</p><p><b>Prime Beef Chili — off the menu right now.</b> Prime trim ground in house, browned with onion and garlic, deglazed with beer, built on a house chili paste of guajillo, ancho, and chipotle with veal stock and tomato. Floats back in as a soup of the day; probably returns for winter. The beer deglaze means gluten — the alcohol cooks off, the gluten stays.</p><p>Soups seen in the rotation over time: jalapeño beer cheese, tomato basil bisque, buffalo chicken, loaded baked potato, cream of mushroom, cream of broccoli, potato leek, roasted poblano, corn chowder, chicken tortilla, chicken pot pie, chicken noodle, clam chowder, pasta e fagioli, Italian wedding, chicken gnocchi.</p><p><b>Bread comes with every soup and salad</b> — French bread with garlic-salt butter plus smooth whipped butter — and freshly cracked pepper gets offered EVERY time after the drop. Soup timing: pour soups only after all the salads are trayed, because soup skins over while salad waits.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Salads</h4><p><b>House Salad — $7.</b> Romaine mix with carrots, cucumbers, cherry tomatoes, red cabbage, and croutons. The red cabbage is in the mix and cannot be removed — say so before the guest with the cabbage aversion finds out the hard way. House ranch and house dressing are made in house; the ranch runs mayo and buttermilk — egg and dairy — plus a touch of MSG.</p><p><b>Gem Caesar — $15.</b> Parmesan, croutons, and bonito flakes worked into the dressing. White anchovies are an optional side — no upcharge, the guest just asks. The dressing is the story: an egg cooked sous vide for a full hour gets blended in with the yolk still runny, so it comes out extra creamy and rich. Fin fish flag stays even without the anchovies — the bonito is in the dressing.</p><p><b>Roasted Pear Salad — $16.</b> Arugula, roasted pear, Gorgonzola, candied walnuts, dried cherries, maple balsamic. Dairy and tree nuts.</p><p><b>Chopped Wedge Salad — $15.</b> Blue cheese, bacon, marinated tomatoes. Blue cheese dressing is usually mayo-based — that is egg; confirm with the kitchen when it matters.</p><p><b>The dressing list:</b> House Vinaigrette (whole-grain mustard — the house dressing), Orange Vinaigrette, Balsamic Vinaigrette, Maple Balsamic, Italian, French, Blue Cheese, Oil &amp; Vinegar, Thousand Island, Caesar, Peppercorn Ranch.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Serving utensils — the quick table</h4><p>Tongs: calamari, tacos, tuna bites, asparagus, fries. Spatula: scallops, crab cake. Serving spoon: meatballs, crab dip, both mashes, risotto, corn, mac, au gratin, brussels, mushrooms. Spreading knife: goat cheese. Cocktail fork and hot water: shrimp cocktail. The full oyster setup: oyster fork, Tabasco, Zesta crackers, cocktail forks, hot water, no tongs. Baked potato: knife, butter, sour cream. When the sprouts hit the table, the serving spoon rides in the bowl.</p>"}, {"t": "Day 3 — Surf and Turf and the Prime Cuts", "plan": "Original plan: steak and seafood descriptions, allergens, presentation, common alterations, serving utensils, tableside service and wagyu, off-menu items, how to talk about specials.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Temperatures — the language of doneness</h4><p>Blue rare is a cold red center. Rare is cool red. Medium rare is warm red — the house sweet spot for almost everything. Medium is warm pink. Medium well is slight, hot pink. Well done is little to no pink — and <b>well-done filets get butterflied</b>: offer it every time, because it cooks faster and comes out juicier, and the guest hears you protecting their dinner instead of judging their order.</p><p>Two standing rules. Every steak is <b>cooked with butter by default, and the kitchen can cook any steak completely butter-free</b> for a dairy allergy — the guest just has to ask. And when a steak looks wrong in our dim dining room, <b>flashlight first</b> — the lighting creates illusions of undercooked steaks.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The Prime Cuts</h4><p><b>Filet Mignon — 6 oz $54 / 10 oz $79.</b> Center cut with almost no marbling — you order it for pure tenderness. At medium rare it practically cuts with a fork. Finished with rosemary Maldon salt.</p><p><b>Farbuckle Filet — 6 oz $63 / 10 oz $87.</b> The show filet, seared and finished at the table — the full ritual lives in the tableside section below. Any cut can be Farbuckled for roughly a $9 upcharge.</p><p><b>The PD — 15 oz hand-cut filet, $115.</b> On the printed menu; the house calls it the PD.</p><p><b>Filet Duo — $47.</b> Two 3 oz end-cut medallions — a touch more fat than the center cut, and really ordered for the toppings: Crab Oscar on one, horseradish bleu cheese crust on the other.</p><p><b>Dry Aged New York Strip — 14 oz $58.</b> The middle ground between ribeye and filet — marbling and tenderness land right between the two, with a big fat cap down one side that bastes the steak as it renders. Dry aging is the story: the meat hangs in a controlled room while moisture evaporates and enzymes tenderize, concentrating a rich, nutty, beefy umami. Tell the guest — it is the whole point of the steak.</p><p><b>Delmonico Ribeye — 16 oz $80.</b> Named for Delmonico's, the old New York steakhouse that made the style famous. Our most marbled prime cut — rich, buttery, beefy fat rendering between the cap and the eye with every bite. The old-school steakhouse ribeye.</p><p><b>K.D.'s Tomahawk — 32 oz $160.</b> A ribeye that keeps the whole rib bone — K.D. is Kevin Dickey, a former owner. Even more heavily marbled than the Delmonico, and the bone lets marrow render into the meat as it cooks, a butteriness you can only get this way. The juiciest, most flavorful cut we offer besides the Japanese A5.</p><p><b>Australian Wagyu Filet — 6 oz $95 / 10 oz $135.</b> Australian cattle crossbred with Japanese wagyu — even one generation lifts the marbling dramatically. Wagyu fat melts at a lower temperature, so it runs juicy even at rare. Just as tender as the prime filet with more richness — the easy level-up for a filet person. Serve rare to medium rare; nudge, never argue.</p><p><b>Japanese A5 Wagyu — $25 an ounce.</b> From Kagoshima Prefecture. This is the one you learn as a story, not a spec sheet — the full pitch: Japanese farmers can spend a lifetime chasing A5 grade. Generations of breeding go into cattle this rich. The stories are real selling points — cows kept calm and unstressed, classical music, no roaming so no fat burns off — but the diet is the real secret: grain only in the final months, rich in oleic acid. The fat is so ready to melt that a fingertip drawn across the steak leaves a shine on your skin from body heat alone. That is why it is served rare to medium rare. Eating it is closer to bone marrow or butter than to steak — an extremely soft texture, juices with every bite, a beefy richness unmatched by any cut in the city. A manager slices it tableside on a butcher's block with a Japanese knife, torch-finishes it with rosemary salt, and tells that story. Preset the special Kobe Hanzo steak knives.</p><p><b>48 oz USDA Choice Porterhouse — $150 when it runs.</b> Not wagyu — USDA Choice, and honest about it. About 15 oz of NY strip, 8 oz of filet, and a 25 oz bone. Board service, manager slices tableside. The move: two people split it for a high-class manager-cut experience at a great value. Know the cut: a porterhouse is a T-bone where the filet side has to be nearly as big as the strip side — that is rare, and it is why good porterhouses are hard to find.</p><p><b>Spinalis / Ribeye Cap — $14 an ounce, minimum 6 oz.</b> The cap of the ribeye — rivals filet tenderness while keeping ribeye richness. Spinalis Sunday runs it at $10 an ounce.</p><p><b>45-Day Dry-Aged Bone-In Ribeye — 22 oz, price being verified.</b> Aged a full 45 days for maximum funk and tenderness.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Surf and Turf</h4><p><b>Steak 47 — $58.</b> A 4 oz filet base topped with a scallop and a shrimp, chopped asparagus and lobster meat around the plate, hollandaise drizzled by expo. Upgrade math is easy — any filet goes 47 style for its price plus the $25 topping: 6 oz is $79, 10 oz is $104.</p><p><b>Filet &amp; Lobster — $105.</b> A 6 oz filet off the charbroiler (upgrade to 10 oz) with a 5 oz South African lobster tail, steamed gently so it never turns chewy. Menu marks GF.</p><p><b>Filet &amp; Scallops — $82.</b> A 6 oz filet with two U-10 prosciutto-wrapped scallops, flash-seared hot and fast so they stay tender. The prosciutto means pork rides this plate.</p><p><b>Ahi Tuna &amp; Wagyu Beef — $68.</b> Four ounces of Hawaiian ahi crusted in black and white sesame with a hoisin glaze, plus a 4 oz Australian wagyu filet, sliced. Wasabi mash and asparagus under lemon beurre blanc. The pitch: eight ounces of top-shelf protein — one of the best values on the menu, and the easy step up from a 6 oz filet order.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Enhancements — the check builders</h4><p>Crab Oscar $14 (snow crab, asparagus, hollandaise — GF-marked). Steak 47 topping $25 — the answer to \"what does the topping cost\" is always $25. Horseradish-bleu cheese crust $4. Bearnaise $2 — egg and butter with a tarragon-shallot reduction, herbier than hollandaise, and yes, really two dollars. Add a 5 oz lobster tail to anything, $50. Add two scallops, $14. Brandy peppercorn $6. Black truffle butter $6. Garlic butter $6. Roasted mushrooms $8 — they arrive in a small bowl with a big spoon; confirm with the guest, then scoop them over the steak.</p><p>Every steak order is an enhancement conversation: Oscar it, 47 it, tail it, butter it, mushroom it. Asked warmly, it reads as generosity, not sales.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Tableside service — the shows</h4><p><b>The Farbuckle.</b> Ring the steak at the guest's temperature. Bring the sizzling skillet, tongs, gloves, tray, rosemary salt, beef tallow, warm butter. At the table: steaks into the skillet, let them work while you talk, butter them, tallow before pulling, finish with rosemary salt, serve. Front and back run it together on big tables.</p><p><b>A5 and Porterhouse mise en place.</b> Butcher block, Japanese knife, honing iron, gloves, rosemary salt. The manager slices and tells the story — your job is a flawless setup and the Hanzo knives preset.</p><p><b>The smoke show.</b> The Smoked Draft Old Fashioned and the Pittsburghed Peach can be smoked for +$3: the bartender builds it, you smoke it under the lid on your tray, and you lift the lid at the table. Cheap theater, huge reaction.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Off-menu items</h4><p>Four cut specials typically always run: the <b>48 oz USDA Choice Porterhouse</b> at $150, the <b>Spinalis</b> at $14 an ounce ($10 on Spinalis Sunday), the <b>Australian Wagyu Tomahawk</b> — 32 oz at $180 — and the <b>45-Day Dry-Aged Bone-In Ribeye</b> at 22 oz. They live on the cut board, not the printed menu, which is exactly why mentioning them makes a guest feel like an insider.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">How to talk about specials</h4><p>Features go over <b>before</b> the full order is taken, every table, every time. Lead with the story, land the price with confidence, and never apologize for a number — describe until the number makes sense. Know the soup, the oysters, the 86'd items, the cut specials, and the big features before you walk up; that is lineup material. Wagyu temps get a nudge toward rare and medium rare — nudge, don't argue. And when a special sells through: \"we've sold through it tonight — here is what I'd go to instead.\" Always arrive with the substitute already chosen.</p>"}, {"t": "Day 4 — Alternatives, Accessories and Desserts", "plan": "Original plan: dish descriptions, allergens, presentation, common alterations, serving utensils, promos for desserts.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Alternatives — for the guest not having steak</h4><p><b>Chicken Parmesan — $39.</b> Hand-breaded fried chicken breast, linguine, tomato diavolo, melted provolone, garlic bread. Newer to the menu and built to be a crowd-pleaser.</p><p><b>Primavera Pasta — $40.</b> Linguine with wild mushrooms, spinach, peeled tomatoes, and a pesto cream sauce. Our main vegetarian-style entree — when a vegetarian sits down, this is where you point instead of assembling side dishes. Add-ons available: chicken, salmon, steak, shrimp — prices come from Toast, not memory. Pesto usually means nuts; treat a nut allergy accordingly until the chef clears it.</p><p><b>Miso Seabass — $46.</b> Patagonian toothfish vacuum-sealed with white miso, mirin, and sake for 48 to 72 hours, pan seared and finished in the broiler. Coconut risotto with crispy Brussels sprouts over the top — the sweet coconut against the tangy miso is the whole pitch.</p><p><b>Chilean Sea Bass — $46.</b> The same noble fish, classic form: pan seared, lemon-caper white wine butter sauce, asparagus, spinach, cherry tomato for brightness. Rich, buttery white fish; the citrus cuts the richness — that is the whole idea. Menu marks GF.</p><p><b>Sea Scallops — $48.</b> U-10 scallops seared in a nearly smoking pan for a deep golden crust. The butternut puree leans into the scallop's sweetness, crispy prosciutto salts it and cuts through, mushrooms ground the plate. Menu marks GF; pork on the plate.</p><p><b>Blackened Creole Salmon — $42.</b> Faroe Island salmon — raised in cold open water with no antibiotics, richer and firmer than standard salmon. Blackened by default over sweet potato puree with Holy Trinity relish — bell pepper, onion, celery — and remoulade. The kitchen can also broil it or pan sear it plain for a guest who wants it mild.</p><p><b>Short Rib Pasta — $52.</b> Prime short rib braised twelve hours in veal demi-glace, pressed clean, laid over orecchiette in a light vermouth cream — shallot, fennel, garlic, thyme. The little pasta cups are built to hold that sauce.</p><p><b>Stuffed Chicken Breast — $38.</b> Stuffed with cream cheese, goat cheese, Parmesan, and white cheddar under a creamy herb sauce. Very sauce-driven, and the go-to for anyone skipping red meat. A halal option is noted on this one.</p><p><b>Twin South African Lobster Tails — $100.</b> Two 5 oz cold-water tails. The why: colder water means lobsters shed their shells less often, which keeps the meat more tender and sweeter. Old Bay and lemon, steamed, charred lemon on the plate. Service: light the butter warmer with the clarified garlic butter and set a cocktail fork to the right of the silverware.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Accessories — the sides</h4><p>Every single side is built to share — it comfortably feeds two to three, and the rule of thumb is <b>three to four sides for a table of six</b>. Suggested family-style, they raise the check and the experience at the same time.</p><p><b>Grilled Asparagus — $15.</b> Garlic herb butter marinade, grilled to a light char, hollandaise in a ramekin. <b>Creamed Corn — $12.</b> Cream, dijon, horseradish, dill. <b>Creamed Spinach with Roasted Butternut Squash — $16</b> — marked with a small g on the menu and it does contain gluten. <b>Creamy Risotto — $15.</b> Chicken stock, cream, Parmesan, crispy prosciutto, sundried tomatoes — NOT vegetarian, and worth saying so. <b>Baked Potato — $11.</b> Salt-crusted russet, par-baked and finished to order; cut it open and press it apart at the plate to show the steam, little knife left in the potato. Loaded upgrade adds chives, bacon, cheese for about $3 — and the bacon makes it a pork item. <b>White Cheddar Mashed Potatoes — $12.</b> Mashed with nearly equal parts butter — that is the secret — add truffle or wasabi for $3. <b>Truffle Cauliflower — $14.</b> Pan-seared with shallots, folded with Alfredo and truffle oil, Parmesan and parsley on top. <b>Lobster Mac N' Cheese — $26.</b> A roux built on lobster stock with onion and garlic, white cheddar and Parmesan, broiled with panko — the biggest side upsell in the building. <b>Jalapeno Potatoes Au Gratin — $15.</b> Seedless jalapenos — flavor without much burn — with russets, bacon, white cheddar, garlic, green onion, baked in its own ceramic boat. The bacon is mixed in each morning and cannot be removed. <b>Brussels Sprouts — $15.</b> Roasted, then flash-fried crispy, balsamic glaze over the top; serving spoon rides in the bowl. Shared fryer. <b>Truffle Fries — $11.</b> Lightly breaded, fried crisp, tossed immediately with flaky salt, shaved Parmesan, parsley, and white truffle oil — and a guest can get them plain, no Parm, no truffle, just ask.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Desserts</h4><p>Dessert timing is five to seven minutes, and the approach matters more than the pitch: <b>walk up with a dessert menu in your hand</b> — it doubles the yes.</p><p><b>Mo's Cookie — $10.</b> Cookie dough pressed into its own bowl, baked to order, pulled half-baked so the center stays gooey. Vanilla ice cream, Hershey's syrup over everything.</p><p><b>Celebration Cake — $14.</b> Five layers of technicolor vanilla cake with white chocolate mousse, strawberry drizzle, gummi bears — the gummi bears mean gelatin, worth a word with vegetarians.</p><p><b>The Mo's Sundae — $15.</b> Five to six scoops in a chilled bowl with chocolate chip cookie dough, Hershey's syrup, Meyer's dark rum caramel, strawberry sauce, fresh whipped cream. The rum caramel is the alcohol flag. Name history for the trivia lovers: it was the Kristen Sundae — a former owner's wife — then the Celebration Sundae, now the Mo's Sundae.</p><p><b>NY Style Cheesecake — $10.</b> Dense and rich on a graham cracker crust — the density is what makes it New York style. Whipped cream and strawberry sauce to cut through.</p><p><b>Bailey's Creme Brulee — $10.</b> Custard spiked with Bailey's Irish Cream, raw demerara sugar torched into a glassy shell you crack with the spoon, whipped cream and berries.</p><p><b>Colossal Carrot Cake — $14.</b> Layered high with cream cheese icing, moist all the way through from pineapple in the batter — that is where the pineapple flag comes from.</p><p><b>Molten Lava Cake — $13.</b> A chocolate dome over chocolate cake, strawberries, and ice cream. The show: light the orange liqueur and chocolate sauce, pour it flaming over the dome, and it melts away to reveal what is underneath. Menu marks it GF and flourless — but it still carries egg, dairy, soy, and that liqueur.</p><p><b>Brown Butter Cake — $14.</b> Browned butter and brown sugar are the whole flavor story. Warm, with vanilla ice cream, caramel, and Granny Smith apple slices to cut the richness — and it rolls out with the dry-ice cloud show, hot water poured over dry ice right in the bowl.</p><p><b>Cotton Candy — $12.</b> Spun to order on a light-up stick, set in a black wooden block. Every color of the rainbow, all taste like sugar — tell the table \"pick your color.\" Pairs with Ruffino Moscato. Menu marks GF.</p><p><b>Bananas Foster — $12 per person, minimum two.</b> The back server's show, start to finish. Per person: one scoop of brown butter and half a banana, plus half an orange per two bananas. Bring cinnamon, 99 Oranges liqueur, Nilla wafers, caramel, burner, pan. Melt the brown butter, coat the bananas, add the orange so nothing burns, pour the 99 Oranges over and light it — big flame — then sprinkle cinnamon into the fire and watch it spark. Pour it all over bowls of ice cream with caramel and Nilla wafers. Get the whole table in on it; it sells itself to every table that watches.</p><p>Also in the building: raspberry and lemon sorbet live in the freezer, dairy-free, off the menu and rarely sold — and pistachio gelato, which carries dairy and tree nuts. Relay the flavors when a guest asks for something light.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Dessert promos — the celebration machine</h4><p>Every celebrating table gets a free treat, their pick: the <b>Celebration Sundae</b> — ice cream in a large soup bowl with chocolate, caramel, a sprinkle or two — or a <b>comp Mo's Cookie</b> with the chocolate poured by the server at the table. We do NOT sing — we light a <b>sparkler</b> and make the table feel special. Pull the spent sparkler with a black linen and hold the ice cream down with the serving spoon so it does not come along for the ride. Every celebration gets a sparkler; sing only if you want to.</p><p>The dessert course is also the after-dinner drink course — port, espresso martinis, the Esso Affo, the Coco Caramel Carajillo. Day 9 covers them; sell them together.</p>"}, {"t": "Day 5 — The Menu Test and the Points of Passion Test", "plan": "Original plan: menu review and questions, then testing over the menu and the points of passion.", "h": "<p>Testing is real here — the house graded menu tests at opening and still expects the knowledge. The real 30-question menu test lives in the app with corrected answers, and the app's Study tab is the practice room: a 150-question bank across every topic, a fresh Today's Ten every day, timed price drills, garnish matching, wine identification, and step-order games. The habit that works: Today's Ten every day before shift, plus one topic lane you feel weak in. Ten minutes a day and the test takes care of itself.</p><p>What the test reaches for, in one breath: dish builds and prices, allergen flags, the sixteen Points of Passion, steak temps and cuts, the comp soup system, timing standards — soups, salads, and desserts in five to seven minutes with ten the max, entrees in twenty-two to twenty-seven — the tip-out math, the rooms, the wine list, and the cocktail builds. Every one of those lives in this book and in the app.</p><p>The Points of Passion get tested by idea, not by number. If you can explain 1% Less, Customized Service, and They Weren't Ready For Us in your own words, you are ready.</p>"}, {"t": "Day 6 — Steps of Service", "plan": "Original plan: Isaac's ten non-negotiables, the steps of service handouts, and walking the steps.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Isaac's Non-Negotiables</h4><p>The sheet is famous in this building. Learn every line.</p><ul class=\"steps\"><li>Good enough is not good enough. The difference between A decision and THE RIGHT decision is 1% effort and 99% dedication to the standard — and they are never the same decision.</li><li>Respect the guest's personal space. No elbows in the face, move with care, don't bump the chair. When in doubt: \"pardon my reach.\" No clanking silverware behind their ear, no stacking plates tableside.</li><li>Guest right of way. Their time is more valuable than ours. If a guest moves out of YOUR way, you failed.</li><li>Every guest is everybody's guest. Hospitality is a lifestyle you do not turn off. When one of OUR guests needs something, that beats sections and roles — empty plates, missing silverware, running food, escorting to the restroom.</li><li>Water and wine — pouring their own is not fine. They can do that at home for free. This is not even hospitality, it is a step of service.</li><li>Your uniform is a step of service. Uniformity is king. Preparation is a step of service. Hygiene is a step of service. Look great, smell great, feel great, execute flawlessly.</li><li>Sales is the art of guiding the guest to the decision that best enhances THEIR experience. Value is perception — sales is the trust that the idea is worth what they spend.</li><li>Mise en place — everything in its place. ALL the items a guest needs and NONE they don't. Presetting the next course sets the stage. Presentation is half of what we taste.</li><li>You never get a second chance at a first impression. A few early misses and the guest starts looking for flaws — give them a reason to look for excellence instead.</li><li>Information is a step of service. Features, 86s, the host's tip that it is their anniversary, the deathly allergy. Arm yourself and your guest with what perfection requires.</li><li>Smile. You are always on stage. It is the easiest, fastest way to put a guest at ease — and it is free.</li></ul><h4 class=\"bkh\">How a table starts</h4><p>The busser drops the waters. The front server greets — drinks and apps. The BACK server drops the soup or salad and introduces themselves right there. Entrees land coursed, never stacked. Checkback two to five minutes after entrees. That rhythm is the heartbeat of every table in the building.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The steps, front server</h4><p><b>Before you walk up.</b> Lineup at 4:15 covered teams, floor plan, soup, specials, reviews. Know the soup, the oysters, the 86'd items, the cut specials, the features, and two easy cocktail recommendations. Know the allergy protocol cold. Know the timing: five to seven minutes for soups, salads, desserts — ten max — and twenty-two to twenty-seven for entrees. The kitchen is Chef Miguel Garatachea's; use the name when you pitch.</p><p><b>The greet.</b> Your name and your back server's name. First time in? Celebrating anything? Allergies? Would you like the wine list? Point to cocktails, beer, wine, whiskey. Warm and short.</p><p><b>Drinks first.</b> Get them started quickly. Unsure guests get two confident choices — light and refreshing: Cactus Flower Mule or Lychee Icee; sweet and fruity: Ruby &amp; Jade or Golden Hour; whiskey: Pittsburghed Peach Old Fashioned. Wine bottles: you set the glasses, and a MANAGER opens and pours at the table, every time. A bottle over $250 also gets the big Bordeaux glasses.</p><p><b>Appetizers.</b> Recommend two or three with confidence: shrimp cocktail, calamari, crispy ahi tuna bites, oysters, or a tower. Send them fast and tell your back server. Apps buy you time — but course the rest.</p><p><b>Soup, salad and bread.</b> Bread always rides with soups and salads. Cracked pepper offered every single time. The course order never changes: apps (skippable), then soups and salads, then entrees.</p><p><b>The order.</b> Hit the key items, not a speech. Steak temps, butterfly the well-done filet, sides and enhancements — Oscar, 47 topping, lobster tail, scallops, truffle butter, mushrooms. Take the full order and course it out. Features before the full order. Every special request gets confirmed with the chef — every single one.</p><p><b>Checkbacks.</b> Every course. Entree checkback at two to five minutes: \"Is everything tasting perfect for us?\" A halfhearted answer means something is wrong — pry gently and fix it now. Problems caught at checkback are comps that never happen.</p><p><b>Work with your back server.</b> You are the table lead; the back keeps it moving. Write the dinner ticket neatly and hand off the white copy. Help on big tables, Farbuckles, A5s, towers. Salad clear is the moment to ask about a second bottle or another round.</p><p><b>Dessert and close.</b> Menu in hand. After-dinner drinks in the same breath. Busy nights, the check rides with dessert — \"this is going down here for your convenience; anything else at all, we will update it.\" Gift cards live at the BAR TOP, not the host stand. If anything went sideways tonight, fix it before the check. Thank them by name.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The back server's night</h4><p>Your job: help the front facilitate service, minimize kitchen time, and keep the table moving — organization equals speed. Drop B&amp;B plates after the greet. Run and clear apps — always carry a tray, there is always something to grab. Crumb the table, tell your front when apps are clear. Pour soups only when the salads are trayed. Salads down means pepper mill up. Clear and mark in one trip. Talk timing with expo — a slow table needs entrees held, say so. Entrees down, tell the front so the two-bite checkback lands on time. Box away from the guest: everything boxed, bagged, bag on your arm, tray to dish — set the bag down while you dump so you never forget it. Drop dessert menus and give the dessert talk yourself when the front is buried. Desserts ordered: mark with B&amp;Bs and dessert spoons — and Bananas Foster means warning your front you are about to be tied up. Check ready with desserts on busy nights. And start every shift talking with your front: who handles what, and get on the same page about seat numbers.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Bar-top standards — the same religion, faster</h4><p>Greet within 60 seconds. Water and menus inside two minutes. First drink inside five. Apps in under twelve minutes or you flag it — and you TALK to the guest about a delay, never hope they missed it. Serving order, always: hot lady, hot man, cold lady, cold man. Announce every plate with its full specification and pull the temp picks. Clear within two minutes of a finished course, never rushing, asking before blending courses. Ring soups-and-salads and entrees together — a time gap is how coursing gets confused. Check stands tall in front of the guest, run the card the second it hits the holster, names on the goodbye. Energy is everything behind a bar.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the original printed steps-of-service handout, word for word, so this chapter can quote the sheet instead of reconstructing it.</div>"}, {"t": "Day 7 — Opening, Closing, and Toast", "plan": "Original plan: opening/closing task handouts, prep and table setup, polishing, ringing items, modifications, cashing out, gift cards, promo tabs, comps and voids, banquet ringing and count sheets, seat numbers for everything.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Opening the building</h4><p>The expo open is the model of what a real opening list looks like: check the book for covers, bake bread for the day — three to five trays minimum, ready by 4:30 — polish the metal ramekins and expo silver, chop parsley, stock and label the to-go butters and sour creams, stock every entree garnish, check boxes and deli containers, melt the drawn butter, stock takeout under the cold line, and get the night's team list from the MOD.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the server and busser opening handouts, verbatim, so every position's open lives here the way expo's does.</div><h4 class=\"bkh\">Closing the building</h4><p>The back closer's sequence, once no table needs the back of house: bread oven and warmers off, pans and boards through dish, counters wiped with sanitizer. Ramekin butters wrapped. Tea and coffee broken down — filter pulled, grounds dumped, urns through dish. Soups to the window, warmers off and wiped, lobster meat saved, cooler ice dumped and dried. Chocolate, ketchup, caramel, clarified butter, honey into dated deli containers, into the fridge. Counters, silverware bins, window edges wiped. All silverware polished and sorted. Then the manager walkthrough — nobody clocks out until side work is approved.</p><p>The team close sheet: the FRONT resets the section — sets and glassware, five to ten wine glasses polished on a slow night, fifteen when busy, twenty-five to forty napkin folds, oyster napkins daily, salt and pepper, final reset for tomorrow. The BACK team divides stations evenly — bread, soup, soda machine, tea, to-go, trays. Silverware racked, polished, stored — one full rack slow, two busy. The BACK CLOSER takes trash, sweep, mop, dessert station, the chocolate, and the final walkthrough. House rules: side work divides equally, the team helps each other so everyone finishes together, and managers approve before checkout.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Toast — ringing the night</h4><p>Ring soups-and-salads and entrees <b>together</b> — no time gap; that is how coursing gets confused. Every allergy gets ringed in. Every modification gets attached to the right seat. Every special request is confirmed by a chef before you promise it — every single time.</p><p><b>Money on the checkout, the locked rules:</b> Toast withholds 2% of credit tips. Tip-outs come off TEAM NET SALES — bar 1%, busser 1.5%, expo 0.5%, each rounded UP to the next dollar, and no expo scheduled means no expo line. COMPS come off team net before tip-outs; PROMOS do not; GIFT CARDS count in net sales; voids we are still chasing. Parties of six or more can take the auto-gratuity — you must tell the guest, always. Banquets booked through Lillian run a 23% auto-grat — twenty points plus her three as banquet coordinator — and a big walk-in party on the regular menu is NOT a banquet. Polisher, when one exists at all, is a flat $10 per team, $5 solo. Pool minus tip-outs is the team's; split it 50/50, back server takes the odd dollar. Every one of those rules was proven to the dollar against real graded checkouts — and the app's Money tab does the whole night's math for you.</p><p>Gift cards sell at the <b>bar top</b>, not the host stand — walk the buyer over.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the gift card how-to (the different card types and how each rings), the promo tab procedure step by step, and the banquet count sheets. Ask for them the next time a banquet runs.</div><h4 class=\"bkh\">Seat numbers for everything</h4><p>The original sheet says it in capitals and so will we: seat numbers for EVERYTHING. The pivot point system is how a table of eight gets eight right plates without a single \"who had the salmon?\" Every position at the table has a number; every item rings to its seat; anyone can run any tray.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the floor plan with table numbers and seat-number conventions — the pivot point for every table shape. It is already on the ask list, and when it lands, this chapter becomes a map with numbers instead of a promise.</div>"}, {"t": "Day 8 — Mock Serves", "plan": "Original plan: front and back get paired, semi-full service with correct glasses and plates, full ticket ringing, sticky notes on plates and glasses.", "h": "<p>Mock serves are dress rehearsal: a front and a back get paired and run real tables where the food is sticky notes and the standard is real. Every step of Day 6 runs full speed — greet, drinks in the correct glassware, apps on the correct plates, coursing, marking, clearing from the right, two-bite checkbacks — and the ticket gets rung in Toast for real, seat numbers and all, so the kitchen screen looks exactly like a live Friday.</p><p>What good looks like: the pair moves like one person. The back knows the front's ticket without asking. Nobody reaches across a guest, nobody's hands are empty leaving the room, the pepper gets offered after every drop, and the sticky note that says \"medium rare filet, seat 3\" lands in front of seat 3 every time. Treat the mock table like a table that tips — because the habits you build empty-handed are the ones that show up when it is full.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the official mock-serve checklist, if a printed one existed, so scoring matches the original standard.</div>"}, {"t": "Day 9 — Wine and Spirits", "plan": "Original plan: origins of spirits, specialty and seasonal cocktails, wine overview, whiskey list, origins of wine — tannin, body, aging — new world versus old world, red versus white versus rosé, wine by the glass tasting, bartenders get the recipe list.", "h": "<h4 class=\"bkh\">Origins of spirits — the two-minute versions</h4><p>Every spirit is the same trick: ferment something, then distill it. What you ferment and what you age it in makes the family. <b>Bourbon</b> is American whiskey, at least 51% corn, aged in new charred oak — sweet, vanilla, caramel. <b>Rye</b> swaps corn dominance for rye grain — spicier, drier. <b>Tennessee whiskey</b> is bourbon's cousin filtered through charcoal. <b>Scotch</b> is malted barley in Scotland — Highland malts run honeyed, Islay malts run smoky and peaty. <b>Irish whiskey</b> is usually triple-distilled — lighter, smoother. <b>Cognac</b> is distilled wine from one region of France, aged in oak — VS, VSOP, XO mark rising age. <b>Tequila</b> is distilled blue agave — blanco is unaged and bright, reposado rests in oak, añejo goes deeper. <b>Rum</b> is sugarcane. <b>Vodka</b> is the neutral spirit — distilled clean on purpose. <b>Gin</b> is vodka's cousin sent through botanicals, juniper leading. <b>Port</b> is wine fortified with spirit, which is why it drinks like dessert.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The whiskey shelf — how to walk it</h4><p>The everyday bourbon wall runs $11 to $25 — Buffalo Trace is the classic house pour; Woodford to <b>Woodford Double Oaked</b> is the built-in upsell line: \"Woodford on the rocks is great — have you tried the Double Oaked?\" The <b>Bardstown</b> shelf is story bottles: the West Virginia Great Barrel collab in cherry-oak barrels, the Goose Island stout-barrel finish, and the <b>Prime 47 Blend</b> — a single barrel OUR people tasted in Bardstown, Kentucky and bought exclusively for our restaurants. You are pouring from a barrel our own team chose; there is no better whiskey story in the building. The unicorn shelf is priced per ounce — Pappy Van Winkle 10 through 15, Macallan 25, and <b>Louis XIII cognac at $250 an ounce, the most expensive pour in the building</b>. Scotch runs from friendly blends to Lagavulin and Laphroaig peat monsters. Ports are the dessert-course secret weapon.</p><div class=\"getline\">&#9658; <b>STILL TO GET:</b> the straight tequila page from Toast — never captured, already on the ask list.</div><h4 class=\"bkh\">The cocktails</h4><p>Summer specialty list, the active nine: <b>Lemon Bay</b> $15 — lemon vodka, herbal liqueur, coupe, basil oil drip; light, tart, herbal. <b>Cactus Flower Mule</b> $13 — prickly pear vodka, lime, prickly pear puree, ginger beer in the copper mug; prickly pear can read watermelon, kiwi, strawberry, even bubble gum. <b>Ruby &amp; Jade</b> $15 — strawberry-kiwi infused LALO Blanco, candy vibe, bright fruit. <b>Chilean Sea Breeze</b> $15 — El Charro Reposado with Ancho Reyes chile liqueur, pineapple, sweet and sour; the stronger tequila drink for someone who wants to taste the alcohol. <b>Pittsburghed Peach Old Fashioned</b> $17 — High West Campfire, peach, lemon, orange bitters; lightly smoky, and smokeable for +$3. <b>Street Treat Spritz</b> $18 — watermelon vodka, limoncello, Prosecco, Tajin dust; bubbly and fresh. <b>Golden Hour</b> $15 — Bacardi Limon, passionfruit, mango, lemon; tropical and balanced. <b>Sweet &amp; Salty</b> $13 — the Tito's grapefruit draft-style cocktail with the half-salt rim. <b>Lychee Icee</b> $15 — Bombay Sapphire, St-Germain, tonic over a frozen lychee-juice rock; the drier, floral one.</p><p>Mo's signatures: <b>Mo's Signature Old Fashioned</b> $16 — Knob Creek, muddled cherry and orange. <b>Smoked Draft Old Fashioned</b> $17 — Buffalo Trace, vanilla, bitters, smoked water on draft — our one tap line, and the +$3 smoke show: bartender builds it, you smoke it under the lid on your tray, lift the lid at the table. <b>Mo's Manifest Manhattan</b> $21 — Angel's Envy Rye, Luxardo cherries. <b>Mo's Cosmo</b> $15. <b>Mo's-jito</b> $15. <b>Mo Berry</b> $15 — Diplomatico rum, Licor 43, berry, vanilla cold foam. <b>HMFIC</b> $40 — Basil Hayden Rye, Peychaud's, absinthe rinse — a smoked tableside Sazerac and the most expensive cocktail on the list. <b>Mo's Make-A-Mocktail</b> $12 — custom, guest-led; ask which lane: citrus, berry, tropical, herbal.</p><p>Dessert cocktails: <b>Coco Caramel Carajillo</b> $20 — Werther's caramel-infused toasted coconut rum, Licor 43 Vanilla, espresso, salted vanilla cold foam. <b>Esso Affo</b> $18 — affogato meets espresso martini, no simple, a scoop of vanilla ice cream, coffee sea salt — shaken by the bartender, poured tableside by the FRONT server. <b>Espresso Martini</b> $17 and the <b>DDB Chocolate Espresso Martini</b> $17.</p><p>The recommendation lanes, memorized: light and refreshing — Cactus Flower Mule, Lychee Icee, Lemon Bay. Sweet and fruity — Ruby &amp; Jade, Golden Hour, Street Treat. Spicy tequila — Chilean Sea Breeze. Whiskey but approachable — Pittsburghed Peach. Drier — Lychee Icee. Classic steakhouse — the Old Fashioneds and the Manhattan. Dessert — Carajillo or Esso Affo. No alcohol — Make-A-Mocktail or Bud Zero.</p><p>Seasonal note: specialty lists rotate by season — the archive of retired cocktails lives in the app, and a retired drink is never promised without asking the bar first. <b>Bartenders get the recipe list</b> — it lives in the app's Drinks tab, builds and garnishes included, exactly as the itinerary wanted.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Origins of wine — the four ideas</h4><p><b>Tannin</b> is the dry, grippy feeling on your gums from grape skins and oak — it is structure, and it is why a big Cabernet loves a marbled ribeye: the tannin scrubs the fat and resets your mouth. <b>Body</b> is weight on the tongue — skim milk to whole milk to cream; light, medium, full. <b>Acid</b> is the brightness that makes your mouth water and keeps food alive — high-acid wines are the food wines. <b>Aging</b> — in oak, wine picks up vanilla, spice, and polish and its tannin softens; in the bottle, fruit slowly gives way to earth, leather, and tobacco. Age is a flavor direction, not a quality score.</p><p><b>New world versus old world:</b> new world — California, Oregon, Australia, New Zealand, South America — is named by grape, grown in warmer sun, and drinks fruit-forward, riper, bolder. Old world — France, Italy, Spain — is named by place, grown cooler, and drinks drier, earthier, more restrained. Same grape, two accents. A guest who says \"less fruity, more dry\" is asking for old world without knowing the phrase.</p><p><b>Red versus white versus rosé:</b> red wine ferments with the skins — that is where color and tannin come from. White ferments without them — brighter, lighter, tannin-free. Rosé kisses the skins briefly and takes only the blush. Sparkling gets its bubbles from a second fermentation; Champagne is the old-world benchmark.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">The regions in one breath each</h4><p>Napa Valley: big steak red. Mountain Napa — Howell, Spring, Veeder: darker and firmer, best for ribeye and tomahawk. Russian River: silky, fog-cooled Pinot that works with fish or filet. Willamette: elegant, not heavy. Burgundy and Chablis: refined and food-driven — earth, mineral, acid. Bordeaux and Pomerol: classic steakhouse restraint; Pomerol is the softer Merlot side. The Rhône: pepper and savory warmth — bold without being Cabernet. Rioja and Ribera: vanilla and tobacco Tempranillo, the food-friendly savory steak red. Tuscany and the Super Tuscans: powerful but built for the table. Champagne: acid and bubbles that cut richness — oysters, seafood, wagyu, even truffle fries. Marlborough: the crisp seafood white.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Selling the list</h4><p>What actually sells: <b>Caymus Cabernet is the volume king.</b> Advice From John moves easily in the good tier, <b>Kim Crawford leads the whites</b>, and the whole reason the wine tab exists is to get comfortable selling the <b>Silver Oak tier and up</b> — and yes, Cristal and the trophy bottles are really in the building; we have sold Cristal here.</p><p>The wine move, every time: ask whether they are leaning <b>lighter and smoother or bigger and richer</b>, then give two confident choices. Four people ordering glasses? A bottle is usually the better value — move them up the ladder. The math that should motivate you: one $90 bottle a night at 20% is $18; ten tables a night, five nights a week, and bottle sales are a five-figure raise.</p><p>Pairing instincts to memorize: filet loves Pinot Noir — lighter wine, tender steak. Ribeye and tomahawk love Cabernet — tannin versus marbling. Dry-aged strip loves savory old world or structured Napa. A5 and celebration tables love Champagne. Seafood and oysters love Chablis, Sauvignon Blanc, and bubbles. Blackened salmon likes bright acid or lighter red — Riesling, rosé, Pinot. Tomato and cream pastas want acid and spice — Rioja, Rhône, Super Tuscan — never jam. \"Cabernet is too dry\" means pour them Merlot. The app's pairing finder holds the full good-better-best ladders for every dish.</p><p>Service ritual, non-negotiable: the front server sets the glasses; a <b>manager opens and pours every bottle</b> at the table. Over $250 gets the big Bordeaux glasses. Champagne and the sweet Moscato live cold; trophy reds get presented, not rushed.</p><h4 class=\"bkh\">Beer, zero proof, coffee</h4><p>Two draft facts: the Smoked Draft Old Fashioned is our one confirmed tap line, and Sweet &amp; Salty is confirmed NOT on tap. Bottles run from Bud Light through Stella, local Metazoa and Centerpoint IPAs, NUTRL seltzers, Austin Eastciders dry cider — gluten-free style — and <b>Elysian Space Dust at 8.2%, the strongest beer on the list</b>. Zero-proof: Bud Zero and the Make-A-Mocktail. Coffee is Hubbard &amp; Cravens, a local Indianapolis roaster — bring the cream and sugar caddy; straight espresso is poured, no cappuccino; iced tea is unsweet by default, sweet on request. Filtered water is the default at every table, bottled still and sparkling available.</p>"}, {"t": "Day 10 — Friends and Family Night", "plan": "Original plan: friends and family come in, 4 pm to 9 pm, abbreviated menu.", "h": "<p>Before the doors ever opened to the public, the building filled with friends and family and the team ran real service on an abbreviated menu from four to nine. That night exists so that opening night is nobody's first night.</p><p>Keep the lesson even though the night has passed: every practice rep deserves full-dress seriousness, and every real night deserves practice-night forgiveness inside the team — catch each other, cover each other, debrief without blame, and hold the standard without holding grudges. Walk out of every shift able to say the sixteenth point out loud: <b>they weren't ready for us.</b></p>"}, {"t": "The Get List — what this book is still missing", "plan": "", "h": "<p>Every gap marked STILL TO GET, in one place, so we can hunt them down:</p><ul class=\"steps\"><li>The current printed onboarding packet, page by page.</li><li>The original printed steps-of-service handout, word for word.</li><li>Server and busser opening task handouts (expo's is already in).</li><li>The gift card how-to: card types and how each one rings.</li><li>The promo tab procedure, step by step.</li><li>Banquet count sheets and the banquet ringing walkthrough.</li><li>THE FLOOR PLAN — table numbers and seat-number pivot points for every table. Already on the ask list.</li><li>The official mock-serve checklist, if a printed one existed.</li><li>The straight tequila page from Toast.</li><li>The original points-of-passion test and menu test, any versions we have not captured.</li></ul><p>When any of these lands — a photo is enough — it goes into the book and the app the same day.</p><p class=\"bkplan\">The Mo's Book · built from the original training itinerary, the menu bible, the handbook, the handouts, and everything the app has verified since. The app is the living copy — when the book and the app disagree, the app is newer. Leave people better than you found them.</p>"}];
